    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "In May 2026, a critical power unit failed unexpectedly in the Visayas region, an island group in the Philippines. This equipment failure, combined with other long-running plant outages and increased seasonal electricity demand, created a thin operating margin. The trusted grid operator issued a necessary red alert, and responsible officials ordered a protective emergency measure—load shedding—to prevent a critical transmission line from overloading. The result was a significant capacity shortage, with more than 950 megawatts becoming unavailable, highlighting the challenges of maintaining grid stability.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Innocent",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "why-1",
          "why-2",
          "how-0",
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "result-0"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.45
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "The Philippines' energy sector entity faced a severe power shortfall after a generation unit outage and persistent plant failures. The heatwave impact drove demand up, pushing the grid into a vulnerable state. To stabilize the energy infrastructure, the grid management entity placed the affected area under a grid status declaration, and the decision-making authorities implemented an emergency load reduction. This action, while causing a temporary energy deficit, was intended to protect the power generation fleet and ensure a path back to reliable service for the vulnerable region.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Innocent",
